<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trail of Tears</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trail of Tears was the forced removal of Native American nations from their homelands in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> under the Indian Removal Act of 1830. The name refers above all to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> removal of 1838 and 1839, when US troops drove some 16,000 people west, about 4,000 of whom died. The Choctaw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, Chickasaw and Seminole suffered similar removals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears.jpg" alt="Trail of Tears" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en.wikipedia.org · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TrailofTearsMemorial-3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, Chickasaw, Choctaw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> and Seminole nations, later grouped as the Five Civilized Tribes, were living autonomously in what would become the American Deep South. Many of their people had become integrated into the regional economy, including the plantation economy and the holding of slaves, who were also forcibly relocated during the removal. American settlers pressed the federal government to remove them, some encroaching on their lands and others wanting more land made available.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>, who made removal his top legislative priority, won passage of the Indian Removal Act of 1830, which authorised the government to extinguish Indian title to land claims in the Southeast. In Cherokee Nation v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> (1831) the Supreme Court held that the Cherokee were not a sovereign and independent nation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester-v-georgia">Worcester v. Georgia</a> (1832) it ruled that the Cherokee Nation was subject only to federal law, not to the laws of Georgia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Choctaw were the first to sign a removal treaty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-dancing-rabbit-creek">Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek</a>, ratified in early 1831. The Seminole were called to Payne's Landing in 1832; most of their chiefs later renounced the agreement, and in December 1835 Seminoles and Black allies ambushed a US Army company, killing all but three of 110 soldiers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> had already lost most of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> lands by the Treaty of Indian Springs of 1825. The Chickasaw were paid for their lands and moved from 1837, merging with the Choctaw nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>.</p><p>By 1838 about 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> had gone voluntarily. Forcible removal began in May 1838, when General Winfield Scott received the order to relocate the remaining Cherokee, enforcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-new-echota">Treaty of New Echota</a>, which the elected tribal leadership and a majority of the Cherokee never accepted. The people were gathered into camps in groups often over 700, where disease spread quickly. The marchers faced extortion, violence, exposure and starvation, and approximately 4,000 Cherokee died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In all, about 60,000 people were displaced between 1830 and 1850. Exposure, disease and insufficient rations killed up to one-third of the Choctaw and other nations on the march. Historians and the affected nations debate whether the name should cover the whole history of removals or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> removal alone, and historians classify the events variously as ethnic cleansing or as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> language the event is called the place where they cried. In 1987 federal law authorised about 3,500 km of trails, the Trail of Tears National Historic Trail, to mark the removal of 17 detachments of the Cherokee. In 1905 the Court of Claims ruled in favour of the Eastern Cherokee Nation's claim, and 1 million dollars was appropriated for eligible individuals and families.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trail of Tears” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Trail of Tears</b> became a lasting symbol of the injustice of Indian removal, commemorated since 1987 by the Trail of Tears National Historic Trail.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cherokee sang a translation by Samuel Worcester on the <b>Trail of Tears</b>, and it was printed in hymnals given to soldiers in the Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>Rejected by Principal Chief John Ross and most Cherokee, it led to the <b>Trail of Tears</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-29">29 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It led to the forced removals known as the <b>Trail of Tears</b>, in which thousands died.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-28">28 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
